          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-133276800ca1b0fd\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":3,\"performative\":4,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"“I will love you always” presents Gerald’s future and enduring love as true while, more strongly, taking the conventional form of a promise or relational undertaking. His coercion, agony, and probable insincerity do not remove those forces. The words are forced out rather than produced as a reflex-like affective discharge, so E lacks independent support.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The future construction supports both a prediction/avowal of continuing love (T) and a promise-like undertaking (P); the immediate demand to say he will love her forever makes P dominant.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Gerald directly utters the formula, although he is repeating words solicited by Gudrun and does so under intense interpersonal pressure.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Gudrun demands a declaration even if it is false, after Gerald has repeatedly denied loving her or ever doing so. He complies in agony, apparently as coerced verbal submission rather than sincere reassurance, and she immediately treats his saying it with raillery. This undermines sincerity and voluntariness but is compatible with the utterance’s truth-presenting and promise-like force.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Say it, even if it isn’t true—say it Gerald, do.”\",\"supports\":\"Shows that the declaration is explicitly solicited without requiring sincerity; probable falsity does not prevent T or P force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I will love you always,” he repeated, in real agony, forcing the words out.\",\"supports\":\"The future-plus-“always” wording supports an enduring undertaking and future-state presentation; the narration establishes coerced, painful delivery but not expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Gerald first answers “No” when asked whether he has loved her and whether he ever will.\",\"supports\":\"Strongly indicates contextual insincerity or contradiction while leaving the later utterance’s T/P mode intact.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Fancy your actually having said it,” she said with a touch of raillery. He stood as if he had been beaten.\",\"supports\":\"Frames the declaration as compelled compliance and defeat rather than spontaneous emotional expression.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P naturally capture the declaration’s future-state presentation and promise-like undertaking. Coercion, insincerity, and humiliation are contextual qualifications, not missing dimensions of core force; intensity and agony do not independently establish E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
